\section{Introduction}
Carútana (ISO 639-3 \texttt{cru}) is treated in the sources found as a variety closely related to, or a dialect of, Baniwa (Karu group, Arawakan). Its status is contested: Kaufman (1994) treats the Karu varieties as distinct languages, while Aikhenvald (1999) treats them as dialects. No Carútana-specific educational material turned up in the searches; what exists is general reference material on Baniwa.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
None found. No primers, textbooks or school programmes specific to Carútana appeared in search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia, Baniwa of I\c{c}ana: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baniwa_of_I%C3%A7ana}
\item Wikipedia, Karu language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karu_language}
\item Endangered Languages Project entry (Baniwa/Karutana names): \url{https://endangeredlanguages.com/node/88596}
\item University of Missouri DICE document on Baniwa: \url{https://dice.missouri.edu/assets/docs/awarak/Baniwa.pdf}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found in search results.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None found in search results.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Little is documented for Carútana itself. Any curriculum work would likely need to build on Baniwa/Kuripako materials and on fieldwork with the community, after confirming how speakers themselves define the variety.
\end{document}
